| **The shared role map matches backend role names, but auditor/support’s destination is not authorized.** The backend role model lists the 13 role names in `ROLE_ROUTES`, including `ceo`. But the packet maps auditor/support to `/dashboard/ceo`, while that route permits only CEO/super-admin. | Backend [`user.model.js`](https://github.com/walidaOmar/GimbiyaBackendRest/blob/21acaead0d4acb902e1b9696e767c080d565987b/backend/models/user.model.js); frontend `AuthContext.jsx`; proposed CEO/report guards in Step 1. | Step 1: `DashboardRedirect` and CEO/report routes. | High: auditor/support’s index redirect lands at a route that rejects them. | Give both roles an authorized default route, or leave their default unresolved until a real destination exists. Align role map, guards, and navigation. | Direct-route and dashboard-index tests for every backend-supported role, especially auditor/support. |
| **Nested `RequireAuth` guards are viable, but the invalid role destination remains.** The outer dashboard guard authenticates without a role list; child guards independently check `allowedRoles`, render nothing when unauthorized, and navigate to the role destination. This does not make nested guards fail by itself. | `AuthContext.jsx`, `App.jsx`. | Step 1: protected dashboard routes. | High only where a denied guard redirects to an unauthorized destination; otherwise the pattern works for these routes. | Keep the guards, but verify every role redirect against the destination route’s allowlist. | Test unauthenticated, allowed, and denied access through direct URLs and nested routes. |
| **Backend role names match, but packet navigation omits `manager`.** Manager is an actual backend role and is allowed by the packet’s merchant route; the proposed sidebar has no manager entry and falls back to buyer navigation. | Backend [`user.model.js`](https://github.com/walidaOmar/GimbiyaBackendRest/blob/21acaead0d4acb902e1b9696e767c080d565987b/backend/models/user.model.js); frontend fallback in `Sidebar.jsx`. | Step 2: `NAV_CONFIG`. | Medium: a manager can be routed to merchant but see buyer navigation. | Add manager’s intended navigation and test role destinations and menus together. | Render navigation for each supported role, including manager. |
| **Staff/onboarding routes remain directly accessible in production, and some packet pages call routes absent from this backend snapshot.** Sidebar filtering only hides navigation. The packet leaves the client routes active. In this backend snapshot, `GET /api/users/staff`, `POST /api/users/onboard-staff`, and `POST /api/stores/onboard` are not registered; store requests instead use a coordinator-only `/api/stores/requests` route. | Proposed Step 1 routes and Step 2 filters; backend [`user.route.js`](https://github.com/walidaOmar/GimbiyaBackendRest/blob/21acaead0d4acb902e1b9696e767c080d565987b/backend/routes/user.route.js), [`store.route.js`](https://github.com/walidaOmar/GimbiyaBackendRest/blob/21acaead0d4acb902e1b9696e767c080d565987b/backend/routes/store.route.js), and mounted routes in [`backend/index.js`](https://github.com/walidaOmar/GimbiyaBackendRest/blob/21acaead0d4acb902e1b9696e767c080d565987b/backend/index.js). | High if these flows are meant to be disabled; direct access can still render UI, and API requests may fail or disagree with the UI’s apparent capability. | Block disabled UI routes in production, not only sidebar links. Reconcile the UI’s calls with the actual backend endpoints and authorization policy; do not add or assume backend routes. | Production direct-URL tests and API tests for each onboarding/staff operation and role. |
| **Stock paths, payloads, audit fields, and the packet’s listed reasons match the backend snapshot.** The routes are `/api/stock/manifest`, `/adjust`, `/inbound`, and `/audit/:productId`; adjust takes `productId`, `deltaCount`, `reasonCode`, optional `noteText`. The manifest returns `manifest` and `lowStockThreshold`; adjustment returns `stockBefore`, `stockAfter`, `delta`, `reasonCode`, and `isLowStock`; audit returns `entries` with `deltaCount`, `reasonCode`, before/after counts, populated `actorId`, and `timestamp`. `AUDIT`, `DAMAGED`, `RECOUNT`, and `INBOUND` are accepted reasons; `RETURN` is also valid. | Proposed Step 3 | Low contract risk for the legacy Stock API; still not proof of the deployed service’s version. | Preserve the API contract. Consider exposing `RETURN` only if it is an intended UI workflow; don’t invent new reason codes or payload fields. | Backend contract tests for query parameters, valid/invalid reasons, payload shape, and audit fields. |
